.TH INTELGPU 8
.SH NAME
intelgpu \- first steps with an Intel GPU of generation 8
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aux/intelgpu
[
.B -f
] [
.B -g
.I gdir
]
.B probe
.br
.B aux/intelgpu
[
.B -f
] [
.B -g
.I gdir
]
.B blt
.br
.B aux/intelgpu
[
.B -f
] [
.B -g
.I gdir
]
.B peek
.I reg ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Intelgpu
works on an Intel GPU of generation 8 (Broadwell: HD 5500 and kin)
through
.IR gpu (3),
by default
.BR #G/0 ,
before there is a driver: it finds out what the device can be made
to do.
It refuses a device that is not Intel's (vendor 8086) unless given
.BR -f .
.TP
.B probe
prints the device, its bars, the size of the GTT and of the stolen
memory (from the GMCH control register), the private PAT, whether
forcewake is acknowledged, the state of the blitter's ring, the
pipes and planes of the display, and the panel's power.
It writes only the forcewake request (and takes it back).
.TP
.B blt
gives the GPU its first command: it maps a ring page and a status
page into the last entries of the GTT, starts the blitter (BCS) in
ring buffer mode (no execlists), and has it fill a red rectangle at
100,100 of the visible plane and write a sequence number to the
status page.
It waits for that number at most 2 seconds, prints the ring's
registers, and reads the rectangle back through the aperture.
The ring is stopped and the GTT entries are cleared afterwards.
.TP
.B peek
prints the registers at the MMIO offsets given.
.SH EXAMPLES
On the machine with the GPU, the output to a file to look at later:
.EX
	aux/intelgpu probe >/tmp/probe
	aux/intelgpu blt >/tmp/blt
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/aux/gpu/intel/intelgpu.c
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR gpu (3),
.IR gpu (4)
.SH BUGS
Generation 8 only.
A GPU that hangs may need a reset: reboot.
